WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.857
Authority and scope come before collection, sharing, or action. A team should be able to answer basic questions before it begins: who requested the work, what decision it supports, what information is needed, which systems or records are in scope, which jurisdictions or policies apply, who may receive the results, and who must review sensitive steps.

2
00:00:22.977 --> 00:00:29.508
If the answers are not known, that is a coordination issue, not an invitation to improvise.

3
00:00:29.628 --> 00:00:41.873
Jurisdiction is broader than geography. It can include national or regional law, employment rules, contractual limits, sector regulations, government requirements, data residency expectations, evidence rules, partner agreements, and internal policy.

4
00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:52.197
A corporate fraud team, a SOC, and a public-sector coordination office may all use intelligence processes, but their authorities and constraints can differ sharply.

5
00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:58.439
Even inside one organization, separate subsidiaries, business units, or regulated functions may have different rules.

6
00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:08.763
This course does not provide legal advice or jurisdiction-specific procedures. The operational lesson is to recognize boundary conditions and route uncertainty to qualified reviewers.

7
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:15.006
Legal, privacy, compliance, records, HR, policy owners, and governance stakeholders are not obstacles to coordination.

8
00:01:15.126 --> 00:01:30.228
They are part of lawful coordination. They help determine what information may be collected, how it can be retained, which identifiers should be minimized, whether cross-border sharing is allowed, and whether an action requires additional approval.

9
00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:39.736
Scope also protects the quality of analysis. A defined scope keeps the team focused on the decision rather than chasing every interesting lead.

10
00:01:39.856 --> 00:01:46.386
It limits unnecessary exposure of personal data, sensitive business information, partner data, or restricted source material.

11
00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:52.220
It makes escalation clearer because everyone understands what the effort is intended to answer.

12
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.238
When authority is unclear, pause and document the uncertainty. Ask for review.

13
00:01:57.358 --> 00:02:03.073
Record who approved the scope, what limits apply, and what conditions would require reapproval.

14
00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:10.948
That discipline protects the organization, the analyst, the affected people or entities, and the usefulness of the intelligence product.
